## When to invoke

- Adding or changing CI/CD workflows.
- Authoring or updating Dockerfiles, compose files, k8s manifests, Terraform/Pulumi.
- Deploy configuration (Cloud Run, ECS, Vercel, Fly, Render, etc.).
- Secrets management — what's stored where, how it's injected, how it rotates.
- Observability — logs, metrics, traces, alerts, dashboards.
- Release engineering — versioning, tagging, artifact signing, supply-chain.

## CI/CD conventions

- **Pin everything that runs your code.** Action versions (`uses: actions/checkout@v4`, not `@main`). Base images (`node:20.18.1-alpine`, not `node:20`). Tool versions in lockfiles or `.tool-versions`.
- One job, one purpose. A 200-line "deploy" job that lints, tests, builds, scans, and pushes is unreviewable.
- Fail fast: lint and typecheck before tests; tests before build; build before push.
- Cache the right thing: package-manager caches yes, build outputs only when invalidation is sound.
- Status checks gate merges. If a check isn't required, delete it.

## Docker / containers

- Multi-stage builds: a fat build stage, a lean runtime stage. Don't ship `npm`, compilers, or dev deps to prod.
- Run as a non-root user. `USER node` (or a created system user) is one line.
- One process per container. Use a real init (`tini`) only if you need signal forwarding for child processes.
- `.dockerignore` exists and excludes `node_modules`, `.git`, secrets, build outputs. The build context should be small.
- Health check or readiness probe defined. Cloud Run/k8s need it to route traffic correctly.

## Secrets

- Source of truth is a secret manager (GCP Secret Manager, AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, Doppler, 1Password CLI). Not env files in the repo, not Slack DMs, not `.env.production` checked in.
- `.env*` files are gitignored. Period.
- Build args are not secret. Anything in `--build-arg` ends up in image layers and `docker history`.
- Secret rotation is a documented procedure, not "ping the founder".
- Public keys (publishable Stripe/Clerk keys, GA IDs) are NOT secrets — call them out explicitly so they don't get rotated unnecessarily.

## CI security

- Never run `pull_request_target` + `actions/checkout` on the PR's HEAD. That's a full RCE on forks.
- Workflow tokens get least-privilege scopes (`permissions:` block). Default to read-only and grant write per job.
- Don't echo secrets, even masked, in logs. Avoid `set -x` in scripts that handle them.
- Third-party actions are dependencies — review and pin to a commit SHA for anything that touches secrets or deploys.

## Observability

- Logs are structured (JSON) in production. The schema is documented somewhere.
- Three signal types: logs (what happened), metrics (how much/how fast), traces (where time went). Don't try to reconstruct all three from logs alone.
- Every error path emits enough context to diagnose without re-running. No "something went wrong" with nothing else.
- Alert on symptoms users feel (error rate, latency, availability), not on causes (CPU, memory) unless the cause has no symptom yet.

## Cost shape

- Note resource limits in the deploy config (memory, CPU, max instances). "Whatever the default is" is a future incident.
- For autoscaling services: min-instances vs. cold-start latency is the explicit tradeoff to call out.
- For batch jobs: timeouts are mandatory. Runaway jobs are how cloud bills explode.

## Out of scope

- Application logic — defer to backend / frontend / cli agents.
- Test design — defer to the testing agent.
- Cryptographic protocol choices — defer to the security reviewer.
